            <ArticleTitle>Pearls and pitfalls in first-trimester obstetric sonography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>176-85</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clinimag.2014.10.009</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0899-7071(14)00254-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ultrasound is the primary imaging modality used in the evaluation of first-trimester vaginal bleeding and pelvic pain. This article will summarize the ultrasound findings in normal first-trimester pregnancy, failed pregnancy, ectopic pregnancy, subchorionic hemorrhage, retained products of conception, and gestational trophoblastic disease. Mastery of the spectrum of sonographic findings in the normal and abnormal first-trimester pregnancy allows the radiologist to make accurate diagnoses and helps to appropriately guide patient management.</AbstractText>
